  "title": "EEG-Asymmetries Dataset",
  "description": "EEG recordings from 100 healthy adults collected to study hemispheric asymmetries, specifically the relationship between N1 lateralization and alpha asymmetry. Each participant completed an eight-minute resting-state session with alternating eyes-open and eyes-closed periods. They then completed four lateralized visual tasks (words, faces, emotions, houses) with left- and right-hand responses.",
  "methods_description": "Participants completed a resting-state recording of about 8 minutes (eyes open and closed periods of 63 s each), followed by four tasks in randomized order (words, faces, emotions, houses). Each task had a practice block and separate right-hand and left-hand response blocks (80 stimuli each, 160 total). Stimuli were shown for 1 s, followed by a fixation cross of 500-700 ms. Participants pressed the space bar only for target stimuli (animal names, female faces, houses with pitched roofs).",
